<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
  Copyright (C) 2026 Adam Bogos

  Entitlements for the signed macOS bundle. Notarisation requires the hardened
  runtime, and the hardened runtime is what makes these necessary: unentitled,
  it refuses the executable memory Unicorn's TCG needs and the guest cannot run
  a single translated block.

  Measured, not assumed. A test program driving uc_emu_start over the bundle's
  own libunicorn, signed with the hardened runtime three ways:

    no hardened runtime            JIT OK
    hardened, these entitlements   JIT OK
    hardened, these removed        Could not allocate dynamic translator buffer

  allow-jit is the MAP_JIT path — the sanctioned one, where a region is mapped
  once and flipped between writable and executable per-thread. allow-unsigned-
  executable-memory covers the plain RWX mapping QEMU-derived code still takes
  on some paths; Apple discourages it, and it is here because the alternative
  is patching Unicorn's TCG allocator rather than because it is nice. Both are
  permitted by notarisation.

  Nothing else is granted, and specifically NOT
  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation. The hardened runtime turns
  library validation on, and a hardened process refuses any dylib whose Team ID
  differs from its own — which is why a bundle carrying Homebrew's ad-hoc
  signed dylibs dies at dyld with "different Team IDs" before main() is ever
  reached. tools/package-macos.sh re-signs every dylib with the same identity
  as the executable, so validation is satisfied honestly rather than switched
  off. The opt-out is used only to isolate the two failures while testing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
